One regression: `AND hello` → NULL. Why? parseAdjacency: startsClause('AND') → false (operator, not NOT) → loop never runs → q.and([]) → NULL. Previously leading-AND consumption was inside parseAnd which was always called. Now parseAdjacency's startsClause blocks it before parseOr/parseAnd get a chance.

Fix: in startsClause, allow leading operators? No — better: parseAdjacency should skip leading AND/OR operators before the loop, or startsClause should... Actually the leading-operator skip lives in parseOr/parseAnd, which are only invoked if startsClause is true. Solution: in parseAdjacency, before/inside loop, also skip leading explicit operators:

```
function parseAdjacency(field) {
  const clauses = [];
  skipBoosts();
  while (true) {
    // leading explicit operators with no left operand: skip, parse what follows
    ...
  }
}
```

But careful: don't swallow operators between clauses at adjacency level — `hello OR world`: after parseOr(hello world consumed via OR...), pos past world. Fine. But `aa bb OR cc`: parseOr handles bb OR cc. The adjacency loop just needs startsClause. The problem case is only leading operators (query starting with AND/OR) and after '(' — e.g. `(AND hello)`.

Simplest fix: in parseAdjacency, skip stray AND/OR/ANDMAYBE operators when clauses is empty? But also inside: `(hello AND)` → parseOr consumes hello, AND, then parseUnary → RP → nullQuery. Fine, no adjacency involvement.

What about `hello AND OR world`? parseAnd: hello; AND consumed; parseUnary → primary sees OR → nullQuery; loop breaks; parseOr: peek OR → consume → world. Handled within.

So the only unhandled case: adjacency loop entry where next token is AND/OR/ANDMAYBE. When can that happen mid-loop? After a parseOr returns, trailing ORs are consumed by parseOr itself... but `aa OR OR bb`: parseOr consumes both ORs. `aa bb AND OR cc`: parseAnd: bb... wait parseOr(aa): parseAnd → aa; peek bb not OR; return aa. Adjacency: startsClause(bb) → parseOr: parseAnd(bb): peek AND → consume → parseUnary → primary sees OR → nullQuery → and([bb,null]) → bb... then peek OR, not AND, break. parseOr: peek OR → consume → parseAnd(cc) → or([bb,cc]). clauses [aa, or(bb,cc)]. Good.

So mid-loop operator tokens basically can't be AND/OR after a parseOr completes... except `aa OR bb AND` → parseAnd: bb, AND consumed, parseUnary → EOF nullQuery → and([bb, null]) → bb. Fine.

So fix: in parseAdjacency, before the while loop and at each iteration start, skip leading explicit operators? Safer targeted fix: make startsClause-tolerant entry: 

```
function parseAdjacency(field) {
  const clauses = [];
  skipBoosts();
  // Leading explicit operators with no left operand: just parse what follows.
  while (peekWordIn(OP_AND) || peekWordIn(OP_OR) || peekWord(OP_ANDMAYBE)) pos++;
  while (startsClause()) { clauses.push(parseOr(field)); skipBoosts(); }
  return q.and(clauses);
